# text = The sadness mingled with dignity with which Nydia uttered these simple words , affected the beautiful Ione : she bent down and kissed her The Thessalian kissed the hand of Ione , and then said , with some embarrassment replied the Neapolitan said Nydia She did not wait for the answer of Ione , but , as she spoke , gently and slowly passed her hand over the bending and half-averted features of the Greek -- features which but one image in the world can yet depicture and recall -- that image is the mutilated , but all-wondrous , statue in her native city -- her own Neapolis -- that Parian face , before which all the beauty of the Florentine Venus is poor and earthly -- that aspect so full of harmony -- of youth -- of genius -- of the soul -- which modern critics have supposed the representation of Psyche .
